WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.344
Parallel tool calls.

2
00:00:01.624 --> 00:00:10.157
When the next steps don’t depend on each other, the model can request several tools in one turn: one assistant message with several tool_use blocks.

3
00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:14.789
Claude does this by default, and it is one of the cheapest speedups available.

4
00:00:15.069 --> 00:00:27.933
Anthropic’s multi-agent research post reports that having subagents call tools in parallel, together with running subagents in parallel, cut research time by up to 90% for complex queries.

5
00:00:28.213 --> 00:00:29.643
Your loop has two jobs.

6
00:00:29.923 --> 00:00:38.413
First, run every requested call, and send all the results back in one user message, each tool_result matched to its tool_use_id.

7
00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:46.416
The parallel tool use docs warn that splitting results across several messages teaches the model to stop making parallel calls.

8
00:00:46.696 --> 00:00:55.933
A failed call still gets its result, with is_error: true; never drop one, because a tool_use without a matching result is an invalid conversation.

9
00:00:56.213 --> 00:00:59.349
Second, decide what may actually run at the same time.

10
00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:02.403
Read-only tools are safe to run concurrently.

11
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:06.821
Tools with side effects, or that depend on order, are safer in sequence.

12
00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:25.000
The Claude Agent SDK encodes exactly this rule: its read-only tools ( Read, Glob, Grep, and MCP tools marked read-only) can run concurrently, while Edit, Write, and Bash run one at a time, and custom tools run sequentially unless you mark them read-only.

13
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:33.515
If a task must not fan out at all, disable_parallel_tool_use with tool_choice of auto limits the model to at most one call per turn.

14
00:01:33.795 --> 00:01:38.915
For your trace, log each call in a parallel batch separately but with the same step number.

15
00:01:39.195 --> 00:01:45.445
When you analyze runs, “how many calls per step” is a quick read on whether the agent is planning or fumbling.
